Beverly Susan Sontag (born February 21, 1933 – December 28, 2004) was an American literary critic, essayist, and cultural theorist. She was a prominent figure in the New York Intellectuals and a member of the New York School. Sontag is best known for her essays on philosophy, politics, and art, which often explored the relationship between culture and morality. Her works, such as "Against Interpretation" (1966) and "On Photography" (1977), were influential in shaping critical discourse on art and culture. Sontag also directed films, including "Duet for Cannibals" (1969) and "Brother Carl" (1971). Throughout her career, she received numerous awards, including a MacArthur Fellowship and a National Book Award. Despite her controversial views and style, Sontag's work remains influential in academic and intellectual circles.
